;; The CLOSED-DOMAIN DECISION canary.
;;
;; Deliberately the smallest workflow in this tree. It exists to prove one
;; shape end to end, not to be a controller:
;;
;;     trusted state -> finite legal domain -> scoring -> trusted selection
;;                                                             -> journal
;;
;; Three nodes and no model call in the generation sense. The scorer ORDERS a
;; list that :decide/domain wrote down; it never names an option and never
;; returns text that is acted on. A run with no scorer configured still
;; traverses every node and records a deferral, which is why this manifest is
;; safe to reach on a machine with no inference engine present.
;;
;; Not wired into any turn loop. A capability no manifest reaches is a library
;; entry (AGENTS.md), and this manifest is what makes the capability reachable
;; and testable without asserting that any existing workflow should be using it
;; yet. Wiring it into `loop` or `orchestrator` is a separate decision with its
;; own evidence, and making it here as a side effect of building the canary is
;; exactly the kind of scope creep the canary was scoped against.
{:description "Score a closed set of legal actions and record the decision. Trusted code builds the domain and makes the selection; the model only orders what it is given. Defers rather than guessing when the margin is thin, when the candidates are not comparable, or when no scorer is present."

 :input-schema
 ;; What a driver hands this manifest to start a run. Every key is optional:
 ;; a caller that supplies no vocabulary gets the default action set, and one
 ;; that supplies no legality evidence gets an unauthorized domain that
 ;; :decide/score refuses and journals. The scorer is a transient binding
 ;; (never journalled), so it is :any here and absent from every record.
 [:map
  [:decide/vocabulary {:optional true} [:sequential :map]]
  [:decide/legal? {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/all-legal? {:optional true} :boolean]
  [:decide/legality-source {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/legality-revision {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/domain-id {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/domain-revision {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/state-coord {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/authority {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/context {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/scorer {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/scorer-id {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/model-coord {:optional true} :any]
  [:decide/decision-id {:optional true} :any]
  [:branch {:optional true} :map]
  [:turn {:optional true} :int]]

 :cells {:start  :decide/domain
         :score  :decide/score
         :apply  :decide/apply}

 :edges {:start :score
         :score :apply
         :apply :end}

 ;; No :dispatches. Every path through this workflow is the same path: a
 ;; deferral is a RESULT, not a route. Branching on the decision here would
 ;; push policy into the topology, where a later edit could drop the journal
 ;; edge for one branch and leave the other auditable.

 :invariants
 [{:type :must-precede :cell :score :before :apply :enforced true
   :protects "The decision is journalled before anything acts on it. Reversed,
              the record would be written after the effect it is supposed to
              authorise, which is an audit trail that cannot fail closed."}

  {:type :must-precede :cell :start :before :score :enforced true
   :protects "Nothing is scored that was not first established as a legal
              domain. This is the property the whole shape rests on: the model
              can only ever order options that trusted code already accepted,
              so a bad score cannot widen what is possible."}]}
